# Build-time public configuration.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into the client bundle at build time, so
# nothing secret can live here. This file is committed on purpose: a GA4
# Measurement ID is a public identifier that appears in the HTML of every page
# the property tracks, so keeping it in the repo leaks nothing and means a fresh
# clone or a new Vercel project reports to the right property with no dashboard
# setup.
#
# Anything set in the Vercel dashboard takes precedence over this file, because
# Next.js will not overwrite a variable that is already present in the process
# environment. So this is a default, not a lock-in.
#
# Real secrets (if any are ever needed) belong in .env.local, which is already
# gitignored via the "*.local" rule, or in Vercel's encrypted env vars.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO SECRET MAY EVER BE ADDED TO THIS FILE. THIS IS NOT A STYLE PREFERENCE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file is tracked by git. A key committed here is in the repository history
# permanently — deleting it in a later commit does not remove it, and for a
# public or ever-published repo it must be treated as leaked and rotated. Stripe
# and GitHub both scan for key patterns and will disable a committed live key,
# usually after someone has already used it.
#
# So the rule is mechanical, with no judgement call at the moment of writing:
#
#   Does the value have a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix and appear in page source anyway?
#     -> it may live here.
#   Anything else — API keys, webhook signing secrets, HMAC keys, bearer tokens?
#     -> .env.local for local work, Vercel encrypted env for deployments.
#       NEVER here, not even temporarily, not even a test key.
#
# The full list of variables this project uses, what each one is for, and which
# of those two places it belongs in, is documented in .env.example. Copy that to
# .env.local and fill it in; do not extend this file instead.

# Google Analytics 4 — property created 2026-07.
# Conversions and UTM attribution run through this; see lib/analytics.ts.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-96QK8PY0XH

# Meta Pixel — dataset created 2026-10-23 in the Juliana Ads business portfolio
# (replaces 941626968742591, which sat outside it). For the Serás Tú show ads. Public: it
# appears in page source on /artist/* anyway. Loads on the music pages only;
# see components/artist/MetaPixel.tsx.
NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID=1625994739268729

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cal.com public booking identifiers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These pass the mechanical test above: they are NEXT_PUBLIC_, and they appear in
# page source anyway because the embed builds a public cal.com URL out of them.
# The Cal.com API KEY is a secret and lives in .env.local — do not confuse them.
#
# Together these resolve to the pages a student actually books on:
#   cal.com/juliana-beltran/vocal-assessment-online
#   cal.com/juliana-beltran/vocal-assessment-in-person
#
# The two slugs must stay in step with the event types created by
# scripts/calcom-bootstrap.ts (currently event type ids 6654042 and 6654043,
# recorded in .env.local as CALCOM_EVENT_TYPE_ID_*). Nothing checks that coupling
# at build time: rename a slug in Cal.com and the embed 404s silently, so change
# both together and re-run the bootstrap.
#
# Without these three set, the booking step and the post-purchase scheduler
# degrade to an email hand-off rather than rendering a broken empty iframe. That
# is deliberate, but it is a fallback and not the funnel.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CAL_LINK=juliana-beltran
NEXT_PUBLIC_CAL_EVENT_ONLINE=vocal-assessment-online
NEXT_PUBLIC_CAL_EVENT_INPERSON=vocal-assessment-in-person
